#Sprint Planning — Purpose 🎯

            Sprint Planning is the Scrum event where the Scrum Team decides what can be accomplished in the upcoming Sprint and how that work will be done.

            Main purpose

            The purpose is to establish three things:

            Why? — Sprint Goal
            What is the objective/value of this Sprint?
            Example: "Enable users to search properties using advanced filters."
            What? — Selected Product Backlog Items
            Which stories/items should be taken into the Sprint?
            The team considers priority, capacity, complexity, dependencies, and previous experience.
            How? — Plan for delivering the work
            Developers, including QA/SDET, discuss how they will accomplish the selected work.
            This can include development approach, testing approach, automation, dependencies, test data, environments, etc.
            Example from a QA perspective

            Suppose the Sprint has a story:

            "User should be able to filter properties by price range."

            During Sprint Planning, QA may discuss:

            Acceptance Criteria
            Functional test scenarios
            Boundary-value cases
            API validation
            UI automation requirements
            Test data requirements
            Dependencies
            Environment availability
            Regression impact

            The team then decides whether the story can realistically be included in the Sprint.

🎯 Interview-ready answer

            "The purpose of Sprint Planning is to start the Sprint with a clear objective and a realistic plan. The Scrum Team discusses the Product Backlog, selects the work that can be taken into the Sprint based on priority and capacity, defines the Sprint Goal, and discusses how the selected work will be delivered. As a QA, I participate by understanding the acceptance criteria, identifying testing requirements, dependencies, test-data or environment needs, and providing input on the effort and risks involved."